#
# prima-materia — the searches of the protocol on the waiver that runs both
# ways
#
# One work was opened for the examination, outside the three transmissions
# the statement was formed from: the Haṭhayogapradīpikā, which the holding had
# opened before for other verses and whose regimen sets a place built for the
# purpose, hours of the day, a material, a diet and a continence in one
# chapter, so that the statement could be put to all its kinds of requirement
# in one transmission. The protocol is at
# examinations/the-waiver-runs-both-ways-tested.ttl.
#

pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Haṭhayogapradīpikā releases anybody from one of its requirements, and which kind"@en, "Ob die Haṭhayogapradīpikā jemanden von einer ihrer Forderungen entbindet, und von welcher Art"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814), I.11-17, I.56-64 and II.10-14 read continuously with the commentary and III.83-85 read at the place a term search reached; the translations of Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu) and Srinivasa Iyangar (Adyar 1949) at the same verses" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the work releases a practitioner — the one who has attained, or the one who cannot meet the requirement — from any requirement it sets, and whether the requirement released is fixed outside him (a place, an hour, a material) or bears on his body and conduct (a diet, a continence, an avoidance). The question pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays and pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition put to every transmission they cover." ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Devanagari of the text layer, where the OCR keeps sandhi and joins words: क्षीराज्य (kṣīrājya, 3 hits, all at II.14 and I.62), आदौ and its OCR form आदो (ādau, read at I.61), सिद्धे (siddhe, 30 hits, the ones in the stretches read followed), कदाचि (kadācit, 3 hits, one at I.61), निषिद्ध and its OCR form निषिड (niṣiddha, 1 hit, at I.61), शक्त (śakta, 73 hits, of which the one form of incapacity, kartum aśaktaś cet at II.11, was found by reading and not by the search, because the OCR joins it to the preceding word; afterwards the forms मशक्त and अशक्त returned that one place only), लाभे and लामे (lābhe, 0; the material substitute at I.63 stands as गव्यालाम, found by reading), नियम (niyama, 31 hits, every one read at its line, which reached III.83), असमर्थ (0), अभावे (0), असंभवे (0). The English of both translations was read at the verses read and not searched by term." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HathaKsirajyaNiyamaDrdhabhyase, pmc:HathaAdauVarjana, pmc:JyotsnaSiddheAbhyaseKadacit, pmc:HathaVajroliNiyamairVina, pmc:JyotsnaTrisandhyamAsaktasya, pmc:JyotsnaGavyalabheMahisa ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "Found: three releases from requirements on conduct and body, every one on the ground of the practitioner's attainment and every one in the base text — the milk-and-ghee diet lifted once the practice is firm (II.14), the avoidance of fire, women and the road limited to the beginning (I.61, which the commentary spells out as allowing the accomplished householder his own wife in her season), and the rules of yoga, continence first, set aside for the one who knows vajrolī (III.83). And two releases from requirements fixed outside the practitioner, both in the commentary and both for the one who cannot meet them, each with a second route: three junctures of the day for the one unable to practise at midnight (II.11), the buffalo's milk where the cow's is not to be had (I.63)."@en ;
    skos:note "The limits of the search, and the one that matters. The forms of incapacity in this OCR are joined to the words before them, so that a search for the initial vowel misses them: the place at II.11 was found by reading. A release stated in a stretch not read and in a form the terms do not reach would not have been found, and the stretches read are twenty-four verses of nearly four hundred."@en .

pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaMathaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Haṭhayogapradīpikā releases anybody from its hermitage"@en, "Ob die Haṭhayogapradīpikā jemanden von ihrer Klause entbindet"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814), whole text layer by term search, I.12-14 read with the commentary; the translations of Pancham Sinh and Srinivasa Iyangar at I.12-14" ;
    pm:searchedFor "Whether the place built for the purpose that I.12-14 require — the small hermitage, its country, its door, its wall — is lifted for anybody: the accomplished, the poor, the householder, the traveller, or anyone who cannot build or find one; and whether a second route to the practice is given without it." ;
    pm:searchTerms "मठ (maṭha, 20 hits, every one read at its line: the hermitage of I.12-14 and its commentary, the figure of haṭha as a monastery and a tortoise at I.10, the word for asafoetida, rāmaṭha, at I.59, and two fragments of damaged OCR in the fourth chapter and the English appendix), मठिका (maṭhikā, read at I.12), with the terms of absence and incapacity of pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaSearch: अभावे, असंभवे, असमर्थ, लाभे (all 0), शक्त (73 hits scanned for forms of incapacity; the one such form releases the hour at II.11 and not the place). In the English, I.12-14 read in both translations." ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "The hermitage is the one requirement of the work fixed outside the practitioner in the way a built place is, and nothing in the work lets anybody off it, upward or downward. The commentary explains each feature by its use and adds features from the Nandikeśvara Purāṇa; Iyangar's note glosses the fire to be avoided as earthquakes and volcanoes. The same limits hold as for the search above."@en .
